# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <vx/proc.h>

typedef struct vx_proc {
  vx_handle task;
  uint64_t pid;
} vx_proc;

typedef struct vx_spawn_req {
  vx_str path;
  vx_strs args;
  vx_str ns;
  const vx_handle *handles;
  const vx_str *handle_names;
  size_t nhandles;
  uint32_t intent;
  uint32_t flags; // VX_PROC_NEWGROUP
} vx_spawn_req;

vx_status vx_proc_spawn(const vx_spawn_req *r, vx_proc *out);
vx_status vx_proc_exec(const vx_spawn_req *r);
vx_status vx_proc_wait(vx_proc p, vx_instant deadline, vx_arena *a,
                       vx_str *exit);
void vx_proc_close(vx_proc p);
vx_status vx_postnote(vx_proc p, vx_str note);
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx_proc_spawn` runs the program at <path> in the process's namespace as a child (09 §5.8, M6 step 6e4c3).
It reads the image whole, and its interpreter if it is dynamic (ADR-0047), and makes the task with vx-rt's loader; the child is registered with whatever serves `/proc` before it runs, and <*out> is its task handle and its pid, `/proc`'s name for it.

<args> are its arguments, the first the name it sees for itself (vx_args in start(2)); without them it is the last element of <path>.
<handles> move to the child under <handle_names>, whatever happens.
It is also given the caller's standard output, standard error and console, unless <handle_names> name `stdout`, `stderr` or `console`; standard input only when named.
It shares the caller's namespace group (ADR-0009) and environment group (ADR-0044).
<intent> is its first thread's (ADR-0038; 0 for interactive), and `VX_PROC_NEWGROUP` gives it a note group of its own, as Plan 9's `RFNOTEG`.

`vx_proc_exec` runs the program in the caller's place, its task kept (ADR-0012), and returns only if it cannot.

`vx_proc_wait` waits until <p> ends or <deadline> passes (`VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT`), then puts its exit string in <*exit>, copied into arena <a>: `""` for success, the string it ended with otherwise (ADR-0010). <exit> may be `nullptr`.
Waiting does not let the child go: `vx_proc_close` does, and the task's handle with it.

`vx_postnote` sends <p> a note by writing it to `/proc/`<pid>`/note`, as Plan 9's `postnote` does; a note the program does not handle ends it, with the note as its exit string.

# EXAMPLES

```c
vx_str args[] = {VX_STR("ls"), VX_STR("-l"), VX_STR("/bin")};
vx_spawn_req r = {.path = VX_STR("/bin/ls"), .args = {args, 3}};
vx_proc p;
vx_str why;
if (vx_proc_spawn(&r, &p) == VX_OK) {
  if (vx_proc_wait(p, VX_INFINITE, a, &why) == VX_OK && why.len)
    vx_eprintf("ls: %.*s\n", VX_FMT(why));
  vx_proc_close(p);
}
```

